      "plausibility": {
        "range": {
          "min": 0,
          "max": 772.57,
          "basis": "nasa",
          "source_ids": [
            "EV01"
          ],
          "citation": "NASA-STD-3001 Vol 2 Rev F, requirement V2 6006, p. 52: total pressure for indefinite crew exposure is at most 103 kPa (15.0 psia), and a partial pressure cannot exceed the total, so ppCO2 above 103 kPa = 772.56 mmHg (rounded up to 772.57 at 133.3224 Pa per mmHg) is physically impossible. The floor 0 is the definition of a partial pressure. This bounds the physically possible range only; it is not a hazard limit."
        },
        "saturation": {
          "values": [
            0
          ],
          "basis": "nasa_data_statistics",
          "source_ids": [
            "N02"
          ],
          "citation": "Floor only: a reading of exactly 0 mmHg is a clipped sensor floor, because the ISS cabin CO2 in docs/product/nasa-data/habitat-environment/eda_rr1_telemetry.csv (co2_iss, 12,087 rows) never falls below 1,862 ppm and eda_rr5_telemetry.csv (19,160 rows) never below 1,628 ppm. No full-scale value is listed: no NASA or datasheet source for the deployed sensor was read, so none is invented."
        },
        "stuck": {
          "count": 38,
          "min_span_seconds": 11100,
          "basis": "nasa_data_statistics",
          "source_ids": [
            "N02"
          ],
          "citation": "Computed from eda_rr1_telemetry.csv and eda_rr5_telemetry.csv (co2_iss, 300 s samples, 1 ppm steps): outside the two held stretches (1,837 samples at 2,646 ppm from 2014-09-21 04:24 and 2,666 samples at 3,000 ppm from 2017-06-01 05:00) the longest run of identical values is 37 samples (2,443 ppm, 2017-07-04 12:20 to 15:20, 10,800 s). A sensor is stuck from the 38th identical value, 37 x 300 s = 11,100 s after the first."
        },
        "rate": {
          "max_per_second": 0.001734,
          "basis": "nasa_data_statistics",
          "source_ids": [
            "N02",
            "EV01"
          ],
          "citation": "Computed from the same two files: the largest change between consecutive samples is 673 ppm in 300 s (3,000 to 2,327 ppm, 2017-06-10 11:05 to 11:10), 2.2433 ppm/s. Converted to mmHg with the highest cabin pressure NASA allows, 103 kPa = 772.56 mmHg (EV01 V2 6006, p. 52), so the bound is not understated: 2.2433e-6 x 772.56 = 0.0017331 mmHg/s, rounded up to 0.001734. Observed at 300 s sampling."
        }
      }
